---
import Architecture from '../../layouts/Architecture.astro';
import Diagram from '../../components/arch/Diagram.astro';
import Box from '../../components/arch/Box.astro';
import Edge from '../../components/arch/Edge.astro';
import Group from '../../components/arch/Group.astro';
import Label from '../../components/arch/Label.astro';
import Cell from '../../components/arch/Cell.astro';
import Legend from '../../components/arch/Legend.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/arch/Callout.astro';

// Side view of the drum: centre (CX, CY), radius R, window = 2 * arc with arc = 1 rad.
const R = 130;
const CX = 450;
const CY = 210;
const onArc = (phi: number) => [CX - R * Math.cos(phi), CY + R * Math.sin(phi)] as const;
const ticks = [-1, -1 / 3, 1 / 3, 1].map(onArc);

// Three mini boards for the focus modes, three reels each.
const focusModes: [string, number, string][] = [
  ["'reel'", 0, 'weight 0: own centreline'],
  ["'set-lean'", 0.5, 'weight 0.5: halfway'],
  ["'set'", 1, 'weight 1: board centre'],
];

const formulas = [
  ['halfExtent', '(visibleCells * pitch - (pitch - cellMain)) / 2', 'half the length of the window along the strip'],
  ['arc', 'amount * MAX_ARC, with MAX_ARC = 1.0 rad', 'how far round the drum each half of the window wraps'],
  ['r', 'halfExtent / arc', 'the drum radius that makes the half window wrap through exactly arc'],
  ['phi', '(main - halfExtent) / r', 'a flat coordinate as an angle: 0 at the middle, +-arc at the window edges'],
  ['k', 'depth / (1 - cos arc)', 'the perspective strength that makes the window edge shrink by exactly depth'],
  ['scale(phi)', '1 / (1 + k * (1 - cos phi))', '1 at the middle, 1 / (1 + depth) at the window edge'],
  ['main(phi)', 'halfExtent + halfExtent * sin(phi) * scale(phi) / arc', 'the projected position inside the window; a straight line past it'],
];

const modes = [
  ['How', 'Each symbol is handed a quad and keystones its own texture through a small mesh.', 'The whole reel is rendered to a texture every tick and the texture is bent through one mesh.'],
  ['What bends', 'Only textures that own their whole source: generated textures, single images, render textures. Everything else gets an affine fit.', 'Everything inside the reel container: Spine, atlas sprites, text, effects.'],
  ['Cost', 'Nothing extra per frame.', 'One extra render pass per reel per frame, plus a resample that makes hairline art slightly softer.'],
  ['Needs', 'Nothing.', 'builder.renderer(app.renderer); build() throws without it.'],
  ['Hit testing', 'Symbols are real display objects.', 'Symbols are inside a texture; use getCellQuad instead of screen-space tests.'],
];
---
<Architecture
  title="Curvature"
  subtitle="How a flat strip is projected onto a drum, in two modes."
  eyebrow="Drum"
>
  <p>
    A real slot reel is a cylinder. The cell facing you is full size; the
    ones above and below have rotated away, so their far edges sit further
    from your eye, and a camera draws them as trapezoids rather than as
    smaller rectangles. Flat 2D reels are fine for many games, but when a
    design calls for that mechanical drum look, every symbol on the reel has
    to be squashed and keystoned by exactly the right amount, and it has to
    stay that way while the strip scrolls past at thirty cells a second.
  </p>

  <p>
    pixi-reels fakes the drum with one small model per reel, <code>packages/pixi-reels/src/core/ReelCurve.ts</code>, that is a pure
    function: give it a flat coordinate along the strip and it tells you
    where that point lands on the drum and how much it has shrunk. The
    motion layer keeps laying the strip out flat and knows nothing about
    curvature. The curve is then <em>drawn</em> in one of two ways: per
    symbol, by handing each one a four-cornered shape to fit itself into, or
    per reel, by rendering the whole reel to a texture and bending that.
    This page covers the model, the rule that keeps it from corrupting the
    motion layer, the two renderers and where the "camera" sits.
  </p>

  <Callout kind="plain">
    Wrap the visible window onto an imaginary cylinder. The middle cell faces
    you and is drawn at its true size. Cells further from the middle have
    turned away, so they are drawn shorter and narrower, with their far edge
    the narrowest. The motion code never sees any of this; it keeps placing
    symbols on a flat line, and a projection step bends the picture.
  </Callout>

  <h2>The drum</h2>

  <p>
    Figure 1 is the model from the side. On the left is the flat strip the
    motion layer produces, three visible cells and a buffer at each end. In
    the middle is the cylinder, seen edge-on, with the camera off to the
    left; the window's half extent is wrapped onto the arc, so the middle of
    the window is the point of the drum nearest the camera. On the right is
    what the player sees. Notice two things: the middle cell is drawn
    exactly 1:1, and the edge cells are both shorter along the strip and
    narrower at their far edge, which is the keystone shape a cylinder
    really produces.
  </p>

  <Diagram height={400} caption="Side view. The window's half extent is wrapped onto a cylinder; the middle cell (phi = 0) is drawn 1:1 and everything else is pushed through a perspective divide that shrinks it by how far it has receded. On the right, the keystone you see: far edges narrower, edge cells shorter along the strip; the buffers past the window continue on a straight line.">
    <Label x={64} y={42} text="flat strip" anchor="middle" weight={600} tone="fg" />
    {[0, 1, 2, 3, 4].map((i) => (
      <Cell x={40} y={70 + i * 54} size={48} label={['buf', 'A', 'B', 'C', 'buf'][i]} kind={i === 0 || i === 4 ? 'buffer' : 'plain'} />
    ))}
    <rect x={34} y={118} width={60} height={162} rx="2" class="guide-strong" style="stroke-dasharray: 4 3" />

    <Label x={CX - 40} y={42} text="side view" anchor="middle" weight={600} tone="fg" />
    <Box x={130} y={188} w={90} h={44} kind="ghost" title="camera" mono={false} />
    <path d={`M ${ticks[0][0]} ${ticks[0][1]} A ${R} ${R} 0 0 0 ${ticks[3][0]} ${ticks[3][1]}`} class="guide-strong" style="stroke-width: 2" />
    <line x1={ticks[0][0]} y1={ticks[0][1]} x2={ticks[0][0] + 22} y2={ticks[0][1] - 34} class="guide" style="stroke-dasharray: 3 3" />
    <line x1={ticks[3][0]} y1={ticks[3][1]} x2={ticks[3][0] + 22} y2={ticks[3][1] + 34} class="guide" style="stroke-dasharray: 3 3" />
    {ticks.map(([x, y]) => <circle cx={x} cy={y} r="4" class="arch-marker-primary" />)}
    <circle cx={CX} cy={CY} r="3.5" class="arch-marker-muted" />
    <line x1={CX} y1={CY} x2={CX - R} y2={CY} class="guide" />
    <line x1={CX} y1={CY} x2={ticks[3][0]} y2={ticks[3][1]} class="guide" style="stroke-dasharray: 3 3" />
    <line x1={220} y1={210} x2={ticks[0][0]} y2={ticks[0][1]} class="guide" style="stroke-dasharray: 2 4" />
    <line x1={220} y1={210} x2={ticks[3][0]} y2={ticks[3][1]} class="guide" style="stroke-dasharray: 2 4" />
    <Label x={CX - R + 14} y={CY + 22} text="phi = 0" mono tone="faint" />
    <Label x={ticks[3][0] + 16} y={ticks[3][1] + 12} text="phi = -arc" mono tone="faint" />
    <Label x={CX - R + 14} y={CY - 16} text="r = halfExtent / arc" size={14} mono tone="fg" />

    <Label x={630} y={42} text="what you see" anchor="middle" weight={600} tone="fg" />
    <g class="cell cell-buffer"><polygon points="608,70 652,70 654,116 606,116" class="cell-bg" /></g>
    <g class="cell cell-plain"><polygon points="606,120 654,120 660,172 600,172" class="cell-bg" /><text x="630" y="151" text-anchor="middle" font-size="14" font-weight="600" class="cell-text mono">A</text></g>
    <g class="cell cell-plain"><polygon points="600,176 660,176 660,240 600,240" class="cell-bg" /><text x="630" y="213" text-anchor="middle" font-size="15" font-weight="600" class="cell-text mono">B</text></g>
    <g class="cell cell-plain"><polygon points="600,244 660,244 654,296 606,296" class="cell-bg" /><text x="630" y="275" text-anchor="middle" font-size="14" font-weight="600" class="cell-text mono">C</text></g>
    <g class="cell cell-buffer"><polygon points="606,300 654,300 652,346 608,346" class="cell-bg" /></g>
  </Diagram>

  <div class="not-prose my-8">
    <Legend items={[
      { kind: 'subtle', label: 'visible cell', note: 'projected inside the window' },
      { kind: 'muted', label: 'buffer cell', note: 'fills the band the drum leaves short of the window edge' },
      { kind: 'primary', label: 'ReelCurve', note: 'pure projection, no positions written' },
      { kind: 'accent', label: 'renderer', note: 'PerspectiveCell or ReelWarp' },
    ]} />
  </div>

  <p>
    The model has two knobs, both set through the builder. <strong>amount</strong>,
    from 0 to 1, says how much of the drum you see: at 1 each half of the
    window wraps through one radian, about 57 degrees, so the window spans
    nearly a third of the cylinder. <strong>depth</strong> says how much a
    cell at the very edge of the window has shrunk compared with the middle
    one, as a fraction; a depth of 0.5 means the edge cell is drawn at two
    thirds of its true size. Everything else is derived from those two and
    the reel's geometry. Here is the derivation in words, with the table
    below giving each step its formula.
  </p>

  <p>
    First, measure the window. Its length along the strip is the visible
    cell count times the pitch, minus one trailing gap, because the gap
    after the last cell is not part of the window; half of that is the <strong>half extent</strong>. Then decide how far round the drum that
    half window should wrap, which is the <strong>arc</strong>, simply <code>amount</code> times one radian. Those two fix the drum's radius: an
    arc of length <code>halfExtent</code> subtending <code>arc</code> radians
    has radius <code>halfExtent / arc</code>. That means any flat coordinate
    can be turned into an angle, <strong>phi</strong>: how far it is from the
    middle of the window, divided by the radius. The middle cell is at phi 0,
    the window edges are at plus and minus <code>arc</code>, and the buffer
    cells are beyond that.
  </p>

  <p>
    Next, the shrinking. A point on a cylinder that has turned away by an
    angle phi is <code>1 - cos(phi)</code> radii further from the camera than
    the front, so the natural perspective shrink is one over one plus some
    constant times that distance. The constant, <strong>k</strong>, is chosen
    so that the shrink at the window edge is exactly what you asked for:
    solving <code>1 / (1 + k(1 - cos arc)) = 1 / (1 + depth)</code> for <code>k</code> gives <code>depth / (1 - cos arc)</code>. That is why <code>depth</code> is defined as "the shrink at the edge": it is the one
    number a designer can see and judge.
  </p>

  <p>
    Finally, the position. Seen from the front, a point at angle phi on a
    cylinder sits <code>sin(phi)</code> radii from the centre line; multiply
    by the perspective scale and you have where it lands. The result is
    then normalised so that the middle cell is drawn exactly 1:1. The
    alternative, stretching the mapping so the window edges land on the
    window edges, would stretch the middle row along the strip but not
    across it, which reads as a squashed centre. The consequence of keeping
    the centre honest is that the drum's ends fall a little short of the
    window edges, and the buffer cells fill that band; which is why the
    figure's caption says the buffers "continue on a straight line".
  </p>

  <div class="not-prose my-4 rounded-lg border border-border bg-card overflow-hidden">
    <table class="w-full text-sm">
      <thead class="bg-muted/60">
        <tr>
          <th class="text-left px-4 py-2 font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wider text-muted-foreground">Quantity</th>
          <th class="text-left px-4 py-2 font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wider text-muted-foreground">Formula</th>
          <th class="text-left px-4 py-2 font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wider text-muted-foreground">Meaning</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {formulas.map(([k, v, m]) => (
          <tr class="border-t border-border/60">
            <td class="px-4 py-2.5 font-mono text-xs whitespace-nowrap align-top">{k}</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-2.5 font-mono text-xs text-primary align-top">{v}</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-xs text-foreground/80">{m}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    Past the window edge the position continues as a straight line from
    where the edge actually landed, using the slope the curve had there
    (<code>_edgeSlope</code>), while the scale keeps following <code>1 - cos(phi)</code>. That keeps a buffer cell from being a flat
    rectangle sitting next to a hard-curved neighbour.
  </p>

  <p>
    In code, the config is <code>ReelCurveConfig</code>, <code>{`{ amount, depth? }`}</code>. <code>builder.curve(0.35)</code> and <code>curve({`{ amount: 0.35 }`})</code> mean the same thing, and <code>curvePerReel([...])</code> sets one per reel. <code>resolveCurveConfig</code> clamps <code>amount</code> to 0..1,
    defaults <code>depth</code> to <code>amount * 0.5</code>, and caps depth
    below <code>0.9 * cos(arc)</code>. The cap is not cosmetic: past <code>cos(arc)</code> the projection stops being monotonic and the cells
    at the window edge fold back over each other. An arc below <code>1e-4</code> reports <code>isFlat</code>, and the builder then does
    not create a curve object at all, which keeps a flat set byte-identical
    to an uncurved build.
  </p>

  <p>
    The model is bound to real pixel sizes by <code>setGeometry(cellMain, cellCross, pitch, visibleCells)</code>. <code>Reel.setCurve</code> derives <code>cellMain</code> from the pitch
    the reel is actually spinning on minus the gap, so a MultiWays reel is
    measured on its spinning strip, and <code>Reel.reshape</code> re-binds it
    after a reshape. The main entry point for a renderer is <code>quadFor(mainStart, inset)</code>, which projects the rectangle the
    art really occupies rather than the whole cell. For a trimmed atlas
    frame the <code>cellInset</code> (from <code>textureCellInset</code>) is
    much smaller than the cell, and projecting the whole cell would inflate
    the symbol and give it the cell's keystone instead of its own, milder
    one. The quad comes back <strong>view-local</strong>, clockwise from the
    screen's top-left, so art stays upright on a horizontal set. <code>mapMain(main)</code> and <code>scaleAt(main)</code> expose the same
    projection for overlays you draw yourself.
  </p>

  <h2>The invariant: the curve never writes a position</h2>

  <p>
    There is one rule that everything on this page obeys, and it is worth
    understanding why. The motion layer places each symbol by writing one
    coordinate on its view, and the reel later reads that coordinate back
    to work out which seat the view is in (in <code>beginMotion</code>, <code>notifyLanded</code> and <code>_replaceSymbol</code>). If the curve
    moved the view to its projected position, the reel would read a bent
    value and treat it as a flat one, and the error would compound on every
    round trip. So the curve never touches <code>view.position</code>. It
    hands the symbol a quad, and <code>ReelSymbol.applyCellQuad</code> must
    fit the art into that quad without moving the view; the base
    implementation pays for its fit entirely through the pivot.
  </p>

  <Callout kind="why">
    Keeping the projection out of the position is what lets the whole
    motion contract on the <a href="/architecture/motion/">motion page</a> stay true under curvature. Flat positions are the source of truth; the
    bend is a presentation that can be switched on, changed or switched off
    at runtime without the reel losing track of a single symbol.
  </Callout>

  <p>
    Two set-level helpers measure through the same map so your overlays
    agree with the drum. <code>ReelSet.getCellBounds(reel, cell)</code> returns the axis-aligned bounding box of the projected quad; a payline
    drawn through its centre tracks the curve, while a rectangle outline on
    it is a hair loose on the narrow edge. <code>getCellQuad(reel, cell)</code> returns the four corners in <code>ReelSet</code> space, or <code>null</code> on a flat reel. Use the quad for hit areas in warp mode
    and for anything that should sit <em>on</em> the curve rather than
    around it.
  </p>

  <h2>Two ways to draw it</h2>

  <p>
    The model says where every point should go; something still has to
    draw it there. There are two renderers, chosen with <code>curveMode</code>, and the table sums up the trade. Symbol mode is
    the default and costs nothing, but only content that is a texture can
    be keystoned, and only when that texture owns its whole source. Warp
    mode bends anything at all, at the price of an extra render pass per
    reel per frame.
  </p>

  <div class="not-prose my-4 rounded-lg border border-border bg-card overflow-hidden">
    <table class="w-full text-sm">
      <thead class="bg-muted/60">
        <tr>
          <th class="text-left px-4 py-2 font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wider text-muted-foreground"></th>
          <th class="text-left px-4 py-2 font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wider text-muted-foreground">curveMode('symbol'), the default</th>
          <th class="text-left px-4 py-2 font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wider text-muted-foreground">curveMode('warp')</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {modes.map(([k, a, b]) => (
          <tr class="border-t border-border/60">
            <td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-xs font-semibold whitespace-nowrap align-top">{k}</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-xs text-foreground/80 align-top">{a}</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-xs text-foreground/80 align-top">{b}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    Figure 2 is symbol mode, one frame for one symbol. Follow it top to
    bottom: the flat position is written first, as always; the curve
    converts that position into a quad; and the symbol decides what to do
    with it. Notice the fork at the bottom. A texture that owns its whole
    source goes through a small perspective mesh and really keystones.
    Anything else, including the common case of a sub-frame in a texture
    atlas, takes the affine fallback: scaled and positioned to fit the quad,
    but still a rectangle.
  </p>

  <Diagram height={410} caption="curveMode('symbol'), the default. Each cell is projected on its own and costs nothing extra: the flat position is written first, the curve hands the symbol a quad, and a texture that owns its whole source is keystoned through a mesh while anything else falls back to an affine fit.">
    <Box x={180} y={20} w={360} h={60} title="ReelMotion._render()" sub="flat main written first, as always" />
    <Box x={180} y={104} w={360} h={60} title="curve.quadFor(main, inset)" sub="view-local quad, null when flat" />
    <Box x={180} y={188} w={360} h={60} kind="accent" title="symbol.applyCellQuad(quad)" sub="SpriteSymbol, AnimatedSpriteSymbol" />
    <Edge from={[360, 80]} to={[360, 104]} />
    <Edge from={[360, 164]} to={[360, 188]} />
    <Box x={60} y={308} w={260} h={78} kind="primary" title="PerspectiveCell" sub={['PerspectiveMesh 10 x 10', 'setCorners(quad), own UVs']} />
    <Box x={400} y={308} w={260} h={78} kind="muted" title="affine fallback" sub={['atlas sub-frames, Spine, Graphics', 'contain-fit via pivot, no bend']} />
    <Edge from={[360, 248]} via={[[360, 278], [190, 278]]} to={[190, 308]} label="whole-source texture" labelDx={-12} />
    <Edge from={[360, 248]} via={[[360, 278], [530, 278]]} to={[530, 308]} label="otherwise" />
  </Diagram>

  <p>
    The reason for the fork is that a PixiJS <code>Container</code> transform
    is affine: it can scale, move and rotate a cell, but it can never turn a
    rectangle into a trapezoid. Only content that <em>is</em> a texture can
    be keystoned, by drawing it on a mesh whose corners are the quad. <code>PerspectiveCell</code> (<code>packages/pixi-reels/src/symbols/PerspectiveCell.ts</code>) is that
    mesh, shared by <code>SpriteSymbol</code> and <code>AnimatedSpriteSymbol</code>. It allocates its <code>PerspectiveMesh</code> lazily, re-reads the texture on every <code>apply</code> so an animated symbol can swap frames, and rewrites
    the mesh's UVs from <code>texture.uvs</code> on every texture swap,
    because a mesh's 0..1 UVs address the whole sheet where a sprite's do
    not. It pivots the mesh on the quad's centre so a win pulse scales in
    place. <code>canProjectTexture</code> accepts only textures that own
    their whole source: generated textures, single images and render
    textures. <code>SpriteSymbol.onActivate</code> calls <code>perspective.syncTexture</code> so a pooled symbol keeps its quad
    when it is reused, and <code>AnimatedSpriteSymbol</code> pushes every
    frame change through.
  </p>

  <Callout kind="gotcha">
    An atlas sub-frame, the usual production case, cannot be keystoned in
    symbol mode. It still scales and sits correctly in its quad, so the reel
    looks curved from a distance, but each symbol stays a rectangle. If you
    pack your art into an atlas and want true keystoning, use warp mode or
    give the symbols that matter their own single-image textures.
  </Callout>

  <p>
    Figure 3 is warp mode, one frame for one reel. The reel's container is
    taken off the stage and replaced by a mesh; every tick the container is
    rendered into a texture, and the mesh shows that texture with its
    vertices displaced onto the drum. Notice the last box: the symbols
    themselves are never handed a quad in this mode, so there is no risk of
    bending twice, and anything the engine lifts out of the reel draws flat
    above the drum.
  </p>

  <Diagram height={500} caption="curveMode('warp'), which needs builder.renderer(app.renderer). The whole reel is rendered to a texture every tick and the texture is bent, so everything inside follows at one extra render pass per reel per frame; the symbols themselves are never handed a quad.">
    <Box x={160} y={20} w={400} h={60} title="reel.container leaves the stage" sub="ReelWarp takes its place in maskedContainer" />
    <Box x={160} y={104} w={400} h={78} title="RenderTexture" sub={['reel box + one slotPitch per strip end', '+ curveBleed per side across it']} />
    <Box x={160} y={206} w={400} h={60} kind="accent" title="renderer.render(container)" sub="every tick, even at rest" />
    <Box x={160} y={290} w={400} h={78} kind="primary" title="MeshPlane 16 x 16" sub={['vertices displaced once per geometry', 'cross toward focus, main through mapMain']} />
    <Box x={160} y={392} w={400} h={78} kind="ghost" title="symbols untouched" sub={['no quads handed out, no double bend', 'lifted content draws flat above the drum']} />
    <Edge from={[360, 80]} to={[360, 104]} />
    <Edge from={[360, 182]} to={[360, 206]} />
    <Edge from={[360, 266]} to={[360, 290]} />
    <Edge from={[360, 368]} to={[360, 392]} />
  </Diagram>

  <p>
    Warp mode (<code>packages/pixi-reels/src/core/ReelWarp.ts</code>) bends
    whatever is inside the reel, Spine skeletons, atlas sprites, text and
    effects alike, with no cooperation from the symbol, and it sidesteps the
    sub-frame problem because a render texture always owns its whole
    source. The texture is the reel box grown by one <code>slotPitch</code> at each end of the strip, since the drum's ends curve away from the
    window and the buffer cells fill that band, and by <code>curveBleed</code> per side across it, for art wider than its cell.
    It is created at the renderer's resolution. Each tick the source
    container is moved to the texture's origin plus that margin, rendered
    with <code>clear: true</code>, and moved back within the same
    synchronous block. The render runs even when the reel is at rest,
    because win pulses, cascade destroys and the spotlight all animate
    symbols on a resting reel, and the texture is all the player sees. The
    mesh's vertices are displaced once per geometry change, not per frame,
    and <code>resize</code> re-measures after a MultiWays reshape.
  </p>

  <p>
    Two things change for your game code in warp mode. Symbols are no
    longer real display objects as far as the renderer is concerned, so
    hit-test through <code>getCellQuad</code> rather than against the
    sprites. And anything the engine <em>lifts</em> out of the reel
    container, <code>unmask: true</code> symbols at rest, the win spotlight
    and pin overlays, is not in the texture and draws flat above the drum;
    the builder tells you so with the <code>warp-skips-unmask</code> notice. <code>build()</code> throws if warp mode is chosen without <code>renderer(...)</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Where the camera sits</h2>

  <p>
    So far each reel has been its own drum with the camera straight in front
    of it. On a five-reel board that looks like five separate cylinders side
    by side. A real cabinet has one drum per reel but one pair of eyes, so
    the outer reels are seen slightly from the side and their cells lean in
    toward the middle of the board. <code>curveFocus</code> controls that
    lean. Figure 4 shows three reels under each setting; notice how the
    buffer cells of the outer reels shift toward the centre line as the
    weight rises, while the middle reel never changes.
  </p>

  <Diagram height={300} caption="curveFocus moves the cross coordinate each reel's cells converge on, three reels shown. At weight 0 it is the reel's own centreline; at 1 it is the middle of the board, so the outer reels lean in and the grid reads as one drum.">
    {focusModes.map(([name, w, note], m) => {
      const gx = 12 + m * 236;
      const gw = 224;
      const y0 = 60;
      const boardCentre = gx + gw / 2;
      return (
        <>
          <Group x={gx} y={20} w={gw} h={250} kind="solid" label={`curveFocus(${name})`} />
          {[-1, 0, 1].map((r) => {
            const colCentre = boardCentre + r * 56;
            const focus = colCentre + w * (boardCentre - colCentre);
            const lean = (focus - colCentre) * 0.35;
            return (
              <>
                <g class="cell cell-buffer"><rect x={colCentre - 18 + lean} y={y0} width={36} height={44} rx="2" class="cell-bg" /></g>
                <Cell x={colCentre - 22} y={y0 + 52} size={44} kind="plain" />
                <g class="cell cell-buffer"><rect x={colCentre - 18 + lean} y={y0 + 104} width={36} height={44} rx="2" class="cell-bg" /></g>
                <line x1={colCentre + lean} y1={y0 - 8} x2={colCentre} y2={y0 + 74} class="guide-strong" style="stroke-dasharray: 3 3" />
                <line x1={colCentre} y1={y0 + 74} x2={colCentre + lean} y2={y0 + 156} class="guide-strong" style="stroke-dasharray: 3 3" />
              </>
            );
          })}
          <line x1={boardCentre} y1={y0 - 12} x2={boardCentre} y2={y0 + 160} class="guide" style="stroke-dasharray: 1.5 4" />
          <Label x={boardCentre} y={y0 + 190} text={note} anchor="middle" />
        </>
      );
    })}
  </Diagram>

  <p>
    The focus is a point on the cross axis that a reel's cells converge
    toward as they recede. In words: start at the reel's own centre line,
    and move toward the centre of the whole board by a weight between 0 and
    1. The three named settings are just weights: <code>'reel'</code> is 0 (every reel is its own drum), <code>'set'</code> is 1 (every reel converges on the board centre, so the
    grid reads as one drum) and <code>'set-lean'</code> is halfway. The
    formula, reel-local, is <code>focusCross = cellCross / 2 + weight * (setCentre - reelIndex * (cellCross + crossGap) - cellCross / 2)</code>,
    and the weights are <code>CURVE_FOCUS_WEIGHT = {`{ reel: 0, 'set-lean': 0.5, set: 1 }`}</code>.
    In warp mode the same focus feeds the vertex displacement, so the whole
    texture leans.
  </p>

  <p>
    Leaning has a side effect on masking. A cell that leans toward the board
    centre crosses out of its own column, and the default per-reel mask, <code>RectMaskStrategy</code>, would clip it at the column boundary. So
    any focus other than <code>'reel'</code> switches the default mask to <code>SharedRectMaskStrategy</code> and logs the <code>mask-auto-shared</code> notice; an explicit <code>.maskStrategy(...)</code> always wins. The focus only has an effect
    alongside <code>curve(...)</code> or <code>curvePerReel(...)</code>. The
    mask side of this is on <a href="/architecture/viewport-and-masks/">viewport and masks</a>.
  </p>

  <p>
    The curve can be changed at runtime. <code>ReelSet.setCurve(input)</code> drives <code>Reel.setCurve</code> on every reel, and <code>ReelMotion.setCurve</code> first flattens every symbol with <code>applyCellQuad(null)</code> before dropping to <code>amount: 0</code>;
    without that, the last quad each symbol was handed would stay on screen.
    The <a href="/recipes/reel-curvature/">reel curvature recipes</a> show
    both modes, the focus settings and a payline drawn on the drum.
  </p>

